A prop the call site leaves out is bound to its declared default when the
optimizer inlines the component.

`inlineComponentCall` splices a component body when binding a written prop
unlocks a further fold. It bound the written props and no others, so a
parameter the call site omitted was left standing as a bare identifier in the
spliced body -- which is the defect its own comment records one case of, and
the fallback both of the lowering's inliners already make.

`markup.listItem` is where it shows. Its body chooses between a bullet and a
checkbox on `task == Task.none`, and `task` is the prop nobody writes; with it
unbound the condition was not const, so html emitted *both* branches and every
numbered list item came out with a stray disabled checkbox beside its ordinal.
The item below it writes no prop at all, is not inlined here, and was right all
along -- which is why this was invisible until a markdown document numbered a
list.

html now renders a list item as an `<li>` of its own and no longer reaches the
library body, so bubbletea is the target holding the subject: it still renders
`listItem`'s own body, whose `if task == Task.none` has to fold.

deny * `type="checkbox"` -- neither item is a task, so the branch that draws one is folded away rather than emitted beside the bullet
deny * `\btask\b` -- the prop is bound at the splice; nothing named it survives

-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"
import markup "sngl:ui/markup"

output {
    none {
        html
    }
    go {
        bubbletea
    }
}

ui.window #home(title="ordinals") {
    markup.listItem(marker="1.") {
        ui.text(value="one")
    }
    markup.listItem {
        ui.text(value="two")
    }
}
